  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nHeavy rainfall and flood/flash flood event still appear on track \neven though there has been a westward shift in the guidance. WPC \ncurrently going with a 55% Moderate Risk for excessive rain Tuesday \nfor the western half of the OK/TX Panhandles (including the \nAmarillo area). WPC has suggested this will likely become a High \nRisk as we get closer to the event. The remnants of former \nHurricane Polo will get caught up in the developing southwestern \nUS trough and be moving across southern and eastern New Mexico by \nTuesday evening. With the westward shift in guidance, and the \npossibility of a dry slot affecting some of the area it is not out\nof the question that some of this excessive rain and flash flood \narea will become focused further west or northwest of the region.\nHigh end totals suggest 3-5\" with lower end totals around 1\".  \nTherefore we will hold off on any Flood Watch for now and re- \nassess the guidance tonight and tomorrow morning to better refine\nthe highest probability area for heavy rain. \n\nBesides the heavy rain and flood potential there is the chance \nfor some isolated severe weather across the area Tuesday night and\nWednesday. Parameters would support some marginal severe weather \nso will maintain situational awareness on this aspect of the \nstorm.... but to be clear the heavy rain and flooding potential is\nthe biggest concern. The trough axis should swing through the \narea Thursday afternoon and this will lead to a drier and cooler \nday Friday with highs only in the upper 60s to low 70s across the \nregion.",
